# <%= current_page.data.title %>

[Trunk-based development](https://trunkbaseddevelopment.com/) is the branching strategy for HMCTS repositories. It is what makes [continuous delivery](../principles/continuous-delivery.html) possible:

- all changes integrate into the trunk (`main` or `master`)
- if branches from the trunk are used:
  - they originate from the trunk
  - they re-integrate to the trunk through a [pull request](pull-requests.html)
  - they are short-lived and removed after the merge

Work that is not ready for users is merged behind a [feature flag](feature-flags.html) rather than held on a long-lived branch.

Git-flow is [not recommended](https://nvie.com/posts/a-successful-git-branching-model/), even by its original author. Long-lived `develop`, `release` and `hotfix` branches delay integration and make every merge larger and riskier.

## Release branches

Some release processes need a branch that names exactly what was released. Keep a release branch as close to a tag as the process allows:

- cut it from the trunk at release time
- land fixes on the trunk first and cherry-pick them to the release branch, so that nothing exists only on the branch
- merge or delete it once the release is done, rather than letting it become a second line of development

CJS Common Platform (CPP) cuts a `release/<version>` branch for each release. It can take changes during the release window, and those changes are merged back into `main`; see the [CPP path to live](/cjs-common-platform/path-to-live/index.html) for how a change reaches a release.

A library that supports more than one major version at a time may keep a maintenance branch per major version, for example `release/<major>.x`. That is a [versioning](/standards/versioning/) concern rather than a branching one: day-to-day development still happens on the trunk.

## Release tooling is not a branching model

A release tool with "flow" in its name does not make a repository git-flow. CPP's Maven context services use the jgitflow plugin only to bump the version and tag the release on `main`: its `dev/release-<version>` branches are never pushed and its `dev/release` branch is recreated on every run. See [the release flow on `main`](/cjs-common-platform/standards/artifactory/publishing.html#the-release-flow-on-main).
